<header><p class="st-kicker">duchy · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Duchy of Pomerelia</h1><p class="st-meta">1155 CE – 1294 CE (approx.) · capital Gdansk</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The duchy of Gdansk Pomerania under the Samborides, whose end in 1294 led to centuries of conflict between Poland and the Teutonic Knights.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Eastern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-pomerania">Pomerania</a>, around Gdansk on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a> delta, was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boleslaw-iii-wrymouth">Boleslaw III</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> in the early 12th century and governed for him by local nobles. In the mid 12th century these governors, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samborides">Samborides</a>, began to rule on their own account, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambor-i">Sambor I</a> founded the Cistercian abbey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliwa-cathedral">Oliwa</a> in 1186.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a> controlled the mouth of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vistula">Vistula</a>, and Gdansk grew into a busy port with German, Slavic and Danish merchants. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swietopelk-ii-duke-of-pomerania">Swietopelk II the Great</a>, who ruled from the 1220s to 1266, threw off Polish overlordship, took the ducal title and fought long wars against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mestwin-ii">Mestwin II</a>, who died without sons, left the duchy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl-ii">Przemysl II</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-greater-poland">Greater Poland</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kepno">Treaty of Kepno</a> of 1282. After Mestwin's death in 1294, the land was contested, and in 1308 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> seized Gdansk and held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerelia">Pomerelia</a> until it became part of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/royal-prussia">Royal Prussia</a> in 1466.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/duchy-of-pomerelia.jpg" alt="Duchy of Pomerelia" width="320" height="280"><figcaption>Image: Zuber &amp;amp; MariuszR · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Polska_za_W_Lokietka_(1275-1300).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1155 CE – 1294 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>duchy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gdansk</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sambor I, Swietopelk II, Mestwin II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Pomeranian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/poland">Poland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Gdansk Pomerania, Duchy of Pomerania-Gdansk</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1224&amp;focus=duchy-of-pomerelia" aria-label="Duchy of Pomerelia on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5056 312 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Duchy of Pomerelia lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5376 568L5457 692M5376 568L5451 561"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5457 692h0M5451 561h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5376 568h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5376 568h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1224&amp;focus=duchy-of-pomerelia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
